<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newfoundland Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as London and Bristol Company</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> Company, formally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Company, was an English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joint-stock-company">joint-stock company</a> chartered by James I in 1610 to colonise Newfoundland. Its first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-guy">John Guy</a>, founded a settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupids">Cupids</a> in Conception Bay that year, the first English colony on the island. The company struggled with poor returns and conflict with fishermen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and sold parts of its grant to other investors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newfoundland-company.jpg" alt="Newfoundland Company" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P99 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NFLD_London_Bristol_Company.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
